INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$ca9dc00f$SATQ$, $SATQ$a48b7a71-d74f-4589-9010-e1a7a3afac2d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In <span lang="nv">Din&eacute;</span> (Navajo) culture, <em><span lang="nv">ikaah</span></em> (sandpaintings) are created and then erased as part of sacred healing ceremonies lasting no more than a few days, but <span lang="nv">Din&eacute;</span> <em><span lang="nv">hataałii</span></em> (chanter, healer, cultural guide) Fred Stevens developed fixatives to preserve desacralized sandpaintings. While on a US-sponsored cultural ambassadorial trip in Europe and the Americas in the 1960s, Stevens produced several such sandpaintings and gave them to cultural institutions. This may seem in tension with the role of cultural ambassador&mdash;how could static objects authentically represent an inherently ephemeral and dynamic practice?&mdash;but such a view is itself overly object-focused and neglects how Stevens strove to convey exactly those characteristics of <em><span lang="nv">ikaah</span></em> as a cultural practice.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from an art historian would most directly support the claim made in the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ababe987-344d-4e6c-b4a9-f64ad5404442","content":"<p>&ldquo;While Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings are undoubtedly educative for audiences that have little exposure to <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture, they should not be confused with authentic <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em>, which cannot be extricated from a practice that is intentionally and necessarily transitory nor condensed into a single persistent object.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"edb82a79-5c9b-4fc9-9189-338c055afed9","content":"<p>&ldquo;The most compelling way to reconcile the apparent tension between the temporary and performative nature of <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> and the persistent and static nature of Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings is to recognize that Stevens was an ambassador not only of <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture but of a US art culture that tended to value permanent works over ephemeral ones.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"2520ad72-8c74-4cd7-a0c9-f44f017b2e5b","content":"<p>&ldquo;Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings are best understood not as self-contained objects but as reminders of the public creations of the sandpaintings, during which Stevens conducted appropriate <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> rituals and encouraged viewers to closely track his movements and subtle shifts in the sand throughout the process.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"eaf29393-ef28-4c72-ab2c-bc8e28e00b67","content":"<p>&ldquo;It is important to recall Stevens&rsquo;s own actions as a gift-giver&mdash;a temporary and performative role&mdash;of the sandpaintings, for by taking those actions, Stevens publicly enacted the transmission of knowledge of both traditional <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> practices and cutting-edge chemistry (in the form of the preservative additives), a hybrid that reflects the dynamism of <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2520ad72-8c74-4cd7-a0c9-f44f017b2e5b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most directly supports the text&rsquo;s claim that Stevens strove to convey the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> as a cultural practice. The text challenges the characterization of Stevens&rsquo;s preserved sandpaintings as mere &ldquo;static objects&rdquo; that cannot &ldquo;authentically represent&rdquo; a practice defined in part by the absence of an end product; it claims instead that this &ldquo;overly object-focused&rdquo; interpretation overlooks how Stevens preserved the essential characteristics of traditional <em>ikaah</em> practice. This quotation supports the claim by explaining that Stevens&rsquo;s sandpaintings should be understood &ldquo;not as self-contained objects but as reminders of the public creations of the sandpaintings,&rdquo; during which Stevens &ldquo;conducted appropriate <em>ikaah</em> rituals and encouraged viewers to closely track his movements and subtle shifts in the sand throughout the process.&rdquo; The fact that Stevens created the sandpaintings in a public setting and invited viewers to attentively engage with this process supports the claim that he sought to portray <em>ikaah</em> as a dynamic practice rather than as a static product. Further, the inherently impermanent nature of such a performance evokes the ephemerality that characterizes <em>ikaah</em>.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it contradicts rather than supports the text&rsquo;s claim that Stevens strove to convey the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> as a cultural practice. This quotation argues that Stevens&rsquo;s preserved sandpaintings &ldquo;should not be confused with authentic <em>ikaah</em>&rdquo; and emphasizes that authentic <em>ikaah</em> &ldquo;cannot be extricated from a practice that is intentionally and necessarily transitory.&rdquo; This view aligns with the &ldquo;overly object-focused&rdquo; perspective that the text argues against, rather than supporting the claim that Stevens successfully conveyed <em>ikaah</em>&rsquo;s ephemeral and dynamic characteristics. Choice B is incorrect because it focuses on reconciling the tension between ephemeral and permanent forms rather than supporting the claim that Stevens actively conveyed <em>ikaah</em>&rsquo;s ephemeral and dynamic characteristics. While this quotation acknowledges an apparent contradiction, it suggests that Stevens was accommodating US preferences for permanent artworks; it doesn&rsquo;t demonstrate how he communicated the ephemeral and dynamic nature of traditional <em>ikaah</em> practice. Choice D is incorrect. Although the quotation mentions Stevens&rsquo;s &ldquo;temporary and performative role&rdquo; as a gift-giver, it doesn&rsquo;t address how Stevens conveyed the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> itself during his ambassadorial activities. The quotation focuses on Stevens&rsquo;s role in transmitting knowledge and representing cultural dynamism but doesn&rsquo;t specifically support the claim about his efforts to communicate the essential characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> practice. Further, the quotation incorrectly implies that Stevens shared his knowledge of preservative additives; the text doesn&rsquo;t establish that he revealed how the sandpaintings were preserved.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ca9dc00f","external_id":"a48b7a71-d74f-4589-9010-e1a7a3afac2d","vaultid":null,"uId":"e457e10e-c753-4b22-bc29-9f489882cb30","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> (Navajo) culture, <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> (sandpaintings) are created and then erased as part of sacred healing ceremonies lasting no more than a few days, but <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> <em><span lang=\"nv\">hataałii</span></em> (chanter, healer, cultural guide) Fred Stevens developed fixatives to preserve desacralized sandpaintings. While on a US-sponsored cultural ambassadorial trip in Europe and the Americas in the 1960s, Stevens produced several such sandpaintings and gave them to cultural institutions. This may seem in tension with the role of cultural ambassador&mdash;how could static objects authentically represent an inherently ephemeral and dynamic practice?&mdash;but such a view is itself overly object-focused and neglects how Stevens strove to convey exactly those characteristics of <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> as a cultural practice.</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from an art historian would most directly support the claim made in the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ababe987-344d-4e6c-b4a9-f64ad5404442","content":"<p>&ldquo;While Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings are undoubtedly educative for audiences that have little exposure to <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture, they should not be confused with authentic <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em>, which cannot be extricated from a practice that is intentionally and necessarily transitory nor condensed into a single persistent object.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"edb82a79-5c9b-4fc9-9189-338c055afed9","content":"<p>&ldquo;The most compelling way to reconcile the apparent tension between the temporary and performative nature of <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> and the persistent and static nature of Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings is to recognize that Stevens was an ambassador not only of <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture but of a US art culture that tended to value permanent works over ephemeral ones.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"2520ad72-8c74-4cd7-a0c9-f44f017b2e5b","content":"<p>&ldquo;Stevens&rsquo;s ambassadorial sandpaintings are best understood not as self-contained objects but as reminders of the public creations of the sandpaintings, during which Stevens conducted appropriate <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> rituals and encouraged viewers to closely track his movements and subtle shifts in the sand throughout the process.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"eaf29393-ef28-4c72-ab2c-bc8e28e00b67","content":"<p>&ldquo;It is important to recall Stevens&rsquo;s own actions as a gift-giver&mdash;a temporary and performative role&mdash;of the sandpaintings, for by taking those actions, Stevens publicly enacted the transmission of knowledge of both traditional <em><span lang=\"nv\">ikaah</span></em> practices and cutting-edge chemistry (in the form of the preservative additives), a hybrid that reflects the dynamism of <span lang=\"nv\">Din&eacute;</span> culture.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["2520ad72-8c74-4cd7-a0c9-f44f017b2e5b"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most directly supports the text&rsquo;s claim that Stevens strove to convey the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> as a cultural practice. The text challenges the characterization of Stevens&rsquo;s preserved sandpaintings as mere &ldquo;static objects&rdquo; that cannot &ldquo;authentically represent&rdquo; a practice defined in part by the absence of an end product; it claims instead that this &ldquo;overly object-focused&rdquo; interpretation overlooks how Stevens preserved the essential characteristics of traditional <em>ikaah</em> practice. This quotation supports the claim by explaining that Stevens&rsquo;s sandpaintings should be understood &ldquo;not as self-contained objects but as reminders of the public creations of the sandpaintings,&rdquo; during which Stevens &ldquo;conducted appropriate <em>ikaah</em> rituals and encouraged viewers to closely track his movements and subtle shifts in the sand throughout the process.&rdquo; The fact that Stevens created the sandpaintings in a public setting and invited viewers to attentively engage with this process supports the claim that he sought to portray <em>ikaah</em> as a dynamic practice rather than as a static product. Further, the inherently impermanent nature of such a performance evokes the ephemerality that characterizes <em>ikaah</em>.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it contradicts rather than supports the text&rsquo;s claim that Stevens strove to convey the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> as a cultural practice. This quotation argues that Stevens&rsquo;s preserved sandpaintings &ldquo;should not be confused with authentic <em>ikaah</em>&rdquo; and emphasizes that authentic <em>ikaah</em> &ldquo;cannot be extricated from a practice that is intentionally and necessarily transitory.&rdquo; This view aligns with the &ldquo;overly object-focused&rdquo; perspective that the text argues against, rather than supporting the claim that Stevens successfully conveyed <em>ikaah</em>&rsquo;s ephemeral and dynamic characteristics. Choice B is incorrect because it focuses on reconciling the tension between ephemeral and permanent forms rather than supporting the claim that Stevens actively conveyed <em>ikaah</em>&rsquo;s ephemeral and dynamic characteristics. While this quotation acknowledges an apparent contradiction, it suggests that Stevens was accommodating US preferences for permanent artworks; it doesn&rsquo;t demonstrate how he communicated the ephemeral and dynamic nature of traditional <em>ikaah</em> practice. Choice D is incorrect. Although the quotation mentions Stevens&rsquo;s &ldquo;temporary and performative role&rdquo; as a gift-giver, it doesn&rsquo;t address how Stevens conveyed the ephemeral and dynamic characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> itself during his ambassadorial activities. The quotation focuses on Stevens&rsquo;s role in transmitting knowledge and representing cultural dynamism but doesn&rsquo;t specifically support the claim about his efforts to communicate the essential characteristics of <em>ikaah</em> practice. Further, the quotation incorrectly implies that Stevens shared his knowledge of preservative additives; the text doesn&rsquo;t establish that he revealed how the sandpaintings were preserved.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$cac82f9b$SATQ$, $SATQ$bae6bad2-17de-4f8a-aaf2-dffd919ba1df$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b07d3b53-811c-4d9e-b22a-ec2138ca31d8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Biologist Natacha Bodenhausen and colleagues analyzed the naturally occurring bacterial communities associated with leaves and roots of wild <em>Arabidopsis thaliana</em>, a small flowering plant. The researchers found many of the same bacterial genera in both the plants&rsquo; leaves and roots. To explain this, the researchers pointed to the general proximity of <em>A. thaliana </em>leaves to the ground and noted that rain splashing off soil could bring soil-based bacteria into contact with the leaves. Alternatively, the researchers noted that wind, which may be a source of bacteria in the aboveground portion of plants, could also bring bacteria to the soil and roots. Either explanation suggests that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"26d4a1a5-fc4b-47e0-9e9c-c5746a2ecae0","content":"<p>bacteria carried by wind are typically less beneficial to <em>A. thaliana </em>than soil-based bacteria are.</p>"},{"id":"8e5752c5-73b9-462f-adaf-297891dfb5f9","content":"<p>some bacteria in <em>A. thaliana</em> leaves and roots may share a common source.</p>"},{"id":"fef25a7a-1d20-4b8d-9c80-4baf574b0bcd","content":"<p>many bacteria in <em>A. thaliana</em> leaves may have been deposited by means other than rain.</p>"},{"id":"980d7207-312e-4cc9-a6d3-cfb4940c7233","content":"<p><em>A. thaliana</em> leaves and roots are especially vulnerable to harmful bacteria.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8e5752c5-73b9-462f-adaf-297891dfb5f9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both explanations suggest that the bacteria come from the same place: either they come from the ground and make their way to the leaves, or they come from above the ground and make their way to the roots. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never discusses any benefits of any kind of bacteria. Choice C is incorrect. This conflicts with the text. One of the theories is that the bacteria in the leaves were deposited by rain splashing off soil. Choice D is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text only discusses &ldquo;naturally occurring&rdquo; bacteria. It never mentions either the harms or benefits of these bacteria. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-M002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2d839aac-a92b-43df-91a5-64380f602b7f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cac82f9b","external_id":"bae6bad2-17de-4f8a-aaf2-dffd919ba1df","vaultid":"b07d3b53-811c-4d9e-b22a-ec2138ca31d8","uId":"ecdd8820-1cbb-4ec0-b9ff-4ecb59ec89ba","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Biologist Natacha Bodenhausen and colleagues analyzed the naturally occurring bacterial communities associated with leaves and roots of wild <em>Arabidopsis thaliana</em>, a small flowering plant. The researchers found many of the same bacterial genera in both the plants&rsquo; leaves and roots. To explain this, the researchers pointed to the general proximity of <em>A. thaliana </em>leaves to the ground and noted that rain splashing off soil could bring soil-based bacteria into contact with the leaves. Alternatively, the researchers noted that wind, which may be a source of bacteria in the aboveground portion of plants, could also bring bacteria to the soil and roots. Either explanation suggests that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"26d4a1a5-fc4b-47e0-9e9c-c5746a2ecae0","content":"<p>bacteria carried by wind are typically less beneficial to <em>A. thaliana </em>than soil-based bacteria are.</p>"},{"id":"8e5752c5-73b9-462f-adaf-297891dfb5f9","content":"<p>some bacteria in <em>A. thaliana</em> leaves and roots may share a common source.</p>"},{"id":"fef25a7a-1d20-4b8d-9c80-4baf574b0bcd","content":"<p>many bacteria in <em>A. thaliana</em> leaves may have been deposited by means other than rain.</p>"},{"id":"980d7207-312e-4cc9-a6d3-cfb4940c7233","content":"<p><em>A. thaliana</em> leaves and roots are especially vulnerable to harmful bacteria.</p>"}],"keys":["8e5752c5-73b9-462f-adaf-297891dfb5f9"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both explanations suggest that the bacteria come from the same place: either they come from the ground and make their way to the leaves, or they come from above the ground and make their way to the roots. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never discusses any benefits of any kind of bacteria. Choice C is incorrect. This conflicts with the text. One of the theories is that the bacteria in the leaves were deposited by rain splashing off soil. Choice D is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text only discusses &ldquo;naturally occurring&rdquo; bacteria. It never mentions either the harms or benefits of these bacteria. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-M002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2d839aac-a92b-43df-91a5-64380f602b7f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$cae97f58$SATQ$, $SATQ$277bc788-7cff-47b1-8a2f-743e0b3a2537$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$61ffb443-b409-4607-a7a2-9f94cc1963da$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mosses can struggle in harsh desert conditions because these plants require enough sunlight for photosynthesis but not so much that they risk drying out. Researchers Jenna Ekwealor and Kirsten M. Fisher found several species of <em>Syntrichia caninervis</em>, a type of desert moss, growing under quartz crystals in California&rsquo;s Mojave Desert. To evaluate whether these semitransparent rocks benefited the moss, the researchers compared the shoot tissue, a measure of plant growth, of <em>S. caninervis</em> when growing on the soil surface versus when the moss was growing under the quartz rocks. They found that the shoot tissue was 62% longer for moss growing under the quartz as compared to moss on the soil surface, suggesting that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a1c5663a-23dd-4887-b5cd-bfd44990753c","content":"<p><em>S. caninervis</em> is one of the few types of moss that can survive under semitransparent rocks.</p>"},{"id":"9e6763f2-640e-4388-a063-70ffd12e7969","content":"<p>quartz crystals do not transmit the necessary sunlight for photosynthesis in <em>S. caninervis</em>.</p>"},{"id":"f9c9f0c5-7dd1-4ee4-8037-b8b4fbc14c07","content":"<p><em>S. caninervis</em> growing under quartz crystals experience lower light intensity and are thus able to retain more moisture.</p>"},{"id":"765c15f3-7fa6-48fe-8332-fd686a4f85cf","content":"<p>quartz crystals are capable of supporting <em>S. caninervis</em>&nbsp;growth if the crystals are not too thin.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f9c9f0c5-7dd1-4ee4-8037-b8b4fbc14c07"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because &nbsp;it most logically completes the text. The text explains that while desert moss species need sufficient sunlight for photosynthesis, exposure to sunlight can also affect the plants negatively by drying them out. Ekwealor and Fisher&rsquo;s team found specimens of <em>S. caninervis</em> moss growing under quartz crystals that were semitransparent, allowing some but not all sunlight to pass through them, and the shoot tissue of these specimens was longer than that of <em>S. caninervis</em> specimens found growing on the surface that were unprotected by such crystals. Since, as the text explains, the length of shoot tissue is an indicator of plant growth, and since greater exposure to sunlight results in greater loss of moisture, it can be inferred that <em>S. caninervis</em> growing under quartz crystals experience lower light intensity and are thus able to retain more moisture. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention another moss species besides <em>S. caninervis</em> or discuss whether other such species are able to grow under transparent crystals, as <em>S. caninervis</em> is. Choice B is incorrect because, as the text explains, specimens of <em>S. caninervis </em>were found growing under quartz crystals and exhibited more plant growth than specimens growing on the soil surface. This wouldn&rsquo;t have been the case if the crystals transmitted insufficient light for the moss&rsquo;s photosynthesis.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. The text contrasts the growth of <em>S. caninervis</em> specimens found beneath semitransparent quartz crystals with that of specimens found on the soil surface absent such crystals, but it doesn&rsquo;t make a comparison among specimens growing beneath crystals of different degrees of thickness or consider how the relative thickness of the crystals affects the growth of the moss. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-059-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7dada402-a405-4ea1-bc6b-3766f729fefa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cae97f58","external_id":"277bc788-7cff-47b1-8a2f-743e0b3a2537","vaultid":"61ffb443-b409-4607-a7a2-9f94cc1963da","uId":"f6a2d45c-359b-43e1-958d-55d4e1fd4f1a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mosses can struggle in harsh desert conditions because these plants require enough sunlight for photosynthesis but not so much that they risk drying out. Researchers Jenna Ekwealor and Kirsten M. Fisher found several species of <em>Syntrichia caninervis</em>, a type of desert moss, growing under quartz crystals in California&rsquo;s Mojave Desert. To evaluate whether these semitransparent rocks benefited the moss, the researchers compared the shoot tissue, a measure of plant growth, of <em>S. caninervis</em> when growing on the soil surface versus when the moss was growing under the quartz rocks. They found that the shoot tissue was 62% longer for moss growing under the quartz as compared to moss on the soil surface, suggesting that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a1c5663a-23dd-4887-b5cd-bfd44990753c","content":"<p><em>S. caninervis</em> is one of the few types of moss that can survive under semitransparent rocks.</p>"},{"id":"9e6763f2-640e-4388-a063-70ffd12e7969","content":"<p>quartz crystals do not transmit the necessary sunlight for photosynthesis in <em>S. caninervis</em>.</p>"},{"id":"f9c9f0c5-7dd1-4ee4-8037-b8b4fbc14c07","content":"<p><em>S. caninervis</em> growing under quartz crystals experience lower light intensity and are thus able to retain more moisture.</p>"},{"id":"765c15f3-7fa6-48fe-8332-fd686a4f85cf","content":"<p>quartz crystals are capable of supporting <em>S. caninervis</em>&nbsp;growth if the crystals are not too thin.</p>"}],"keys":["f9c9f0c5-7dd1-4ee4-8037-b8b4fbc14c07"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because &nbsp;it most logically completes the text. The text explains that while desert moss species need sufficient sunlight for photosynthesis, exposure to sunlight can also affect the plants negatively by drying them out. Ekwealor and Fisher&rsquo;s team found specimens of <em>S. caninervis</em> moss growing under quartz crystals that were semitransparent, allowing some but not all sunlight to pass through them, and the shoot tissue of these specimens was longer than that of <em>S. caninervis</em> specimens found growing on the surface that were unprotected by such crystals. Since, as the text explains, the length of shoot tissue is an indicator of plant growth, and since greater exposure to sunlight results in greater loss of moisture, it can be inferred that <em>S. caninervis</em> growing under quartz crystals experience lower light intensity and are thus able to retain more moisture. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention another moss species besides <em>S. caninervis</em> or discuss whether other such species are able to grow under transparent crystals, as <em>S. caninervis</em> is. Choice B is incorrect because, as the text explains, specimens of <em>S. caninervis </em>were found growing under quartz crystals and exhibited more plant growth than specimens growing on the soil surface. This wouldn&rsquo;t have been the case if the crystals transmitted insufficient light for the moss&rsquo;s photosynthesis.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. The text contrasts the growth of <em>S. caninervis</em> specimens found beneath semitransparent quartz crystals with that of specimens found on the soil surface absent such crystals, but it doesn&rsquo;t make a comparison among specimens growing beneath crystals of different degrees of thickness or consider how the relative thickness of the crystals affects the growth of the moss. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-059-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7dada402-a405-4ea1-bc6b-3766f729fefa","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959325,"updateDate":1691007959325}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959325, 1691007959325),
